entry.summary:
When learning Spring Boot, one of the most confusing things is seeing controllers written in many different ways. Sometimes a method returns a DTO. Sometimes ResponseEntity<T>. Sometimes void, String, ModelAndView, CompletableFuture, Mono, and more. At first it feels random. But each return type has a specific purpose. This article is a practical cheat sheet to quickly understand the most common controller patterns in Spring Boot REST APIs. A controller handles HTTP requests. Its job is simple: Receive the request Call the service layer Return a response Example: @RestController @RequestMapping("/api/users") public class UserController {
}
Annotation HTTP Method Purpose
@GetMapping GET Retrieve data
@PostMapping POST Create data
@PutMapping PUT Replace/update completely
